    {
      "id": "ES-C304-tranquilo",
      "sequence": 4320,
      "title": "tranquilo — a sea with no wind on it",
      "headword": "tranquilo",
      "romanization": "tranquilo",
      "gloss": "calm — Latin tranquillus, the word for a sea with no wind on it",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:58ffd00243519bcd",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two from last time, before anything new is added."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say it once slowly, then at speed. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "tranquilo — \"calm.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin tranquillus was, before anything else, a word for water: a sea or a lake with no wind moving across it. Flat, and therefore safe to cross."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English borrowed it whole and kept the picture, whether or not anybody notices it: tranquil, tranquillity."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is the most-used answer in this chapter after bien, and it does something the others do not — said to somebody else, tranquilo means \"take it easy,\" a whole sentence in one word."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A man says tranquilo; a woman says tranquila. You met that -o/-a switch in bueno and buena."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can answer the wellbeing question with something other than bien."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "tranquilo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *tranquilo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "tranquila",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *tranquila*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "tranquilo, gracias",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *tranquilo, gracias*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did tranquillus describe first? (Water with no wind on it.) And what does a woman say? (Tranquila.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C304-enfermo",
      "sequence": 4330,
      "title": "enfermo — not firm",
      "headword": "enfermo",
      "romanization": "enfermo",
      "gloss": "ill — Latin infirmus, not firm; the same word as English infirm",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d7715f76257a1e32",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two from last time, before anything new is added."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say it once slowly, then at speed. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "enfermo — \"ill.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin infirmus is in- (\"not\") plus firmus (\"firm, steady\"). Illness named as the loss of steadiness — not pain, not fever, just the going of the thing that held you up."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English borrowed it twice and kept the shape both times: infirm, and the infirmary where the not-firm are put."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Enfermo is the strong one in this chapter. You already have la fiebre and el dolor; this is the word for the state they belong to. And la enfermera — the nurse — is built straight off it: the one who attends the not-firm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two honest answers now, and one of them explains a word you already had."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "enfermo",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *enfermo*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "enferma",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *enferma*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "enfermo — la enfermera, por favor",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *enfermo — la enfermera, por favor*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Take infirmus apart. (In- not, firmus firm.) Which word you already knew is built on it? (La enfermera.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C304-contento",
      "sequence": 4340,
      "title": "contento — held together",
      "headword": "contento",
      "romanization": "contento",
      "gloss": "happy, content — Latin contentus, held together, from continere",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:7bdd7a499de182a8",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
