              "text": "Two from last time, before anything new is added."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say it once slowly, then at speed. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ocupado — \"busy.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin occupare meant to seize, to take possession of — an army occupies ground. English kept it exactly: to occupy, and an occupation."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So ocupado does not say \"I have a lot on.\" It says something has taken hold of me. The work is the one doing the grabbing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is also the word on a lavatory door, and what a Spanish phone line is when it is busy — the seat and the line are both seized."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four honest answers, and one of them is written on doors."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "ocupado",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *ocupado*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "ocupada",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *ocupada*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "lo siento — ocupado",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *lo siento — ocupado*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did occupare mean? (To seize.) So who is doing the seizing when you are ocupado? (The work.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C304-triste",
      "sequence": 4360,
      "title": "triste — the one word that does not change",
      "headword": "triste",
      "romanization": "triste",
      "gloss": "sad — Latin tristis, which also meant harsh and grim before it softened",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:a93a84459d7928b9",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two from last time, before anything new is added."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say it once slowly, then at speed. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "triste — \"sad.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin tristis was wider than the English word. It covered grim, harsh, severe — a tristis judge was a stern one — and only later settled onto sorrow. French carried the softer end into tristesse, which English borrowed without translating."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Here is the thing worth noticing, and it is a rule you have been shown four times now without it being named: tranquilo becomes tranquila, contento becomes contenta, ocupado becomes ocupada. Triste does not move."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Words ending in -e have one form for everybody. That is not an exception to the -o/-a switch; it is the other half of it, and it costs you nothing to learn because there is nothing to change."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five answers — and the fifth quietly teaches the limit of the -o/-a rule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "triste",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *triste*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "triste, gracias",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *triste, gracias*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "contento — contenta — triste",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *contento — contenta — triste*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Did tristis only mean sad? (No — grim and harsh too.) And why does triste not change for a woman? (It ends in -e.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
